+---
+id: dl-001-decline-to-remove-vat-from-shipping-matches-documented-business-rule
+type: decision-log
+title: "Decline to remove VAT from shipping: matches documented business rule"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Anna, a customer, reported that her receipt shows VAT on the delivery cost (€1.08 on €4.90 of
+shipping) and asked that VAT stop being added to shipping, since delivery is a service, not goods.
+
+`README.md`'s VAT section explicitly states: "Shipping is subject to VAT at the standard rate (22%),
+shown on its own line of the receipt." The code (`shippingVat` in `src/tax.ts`) applies exactly that:
+€4.90 × 22% = €1.078, rounded half up to the cent = €1.08, matching Anna's receipt exactly.
+`README.md` also says: "When the code and this list disagree, this list is right" — here the code
+agrees with the list.
+
+## Decision
+
+Treat this report as a request to change a documented business rule, not as a bug, and make no code
+change. Taxing shipping at the standard rate is current, explicit policy; reversing it is a product/tax
+decision outside a bug-fix task, and would need:
+
+- sign-off from whoever owns VAT/pricing policy (the rule may reflect a real tax-law requirement, e.g.
+  shipping often follows the tax treatment of the goods it accompanies, or is taxed regardless), and
+- an update to `README.md`'s VAT section in the same change that altered the behaviour, per the
+  documentation directive.
+
+## Consequences
+
+No code or test changed for this report. Anna should be told her receipt is correct under the shop's
+current, documented VAT rule. If the business decides to change the rule, that is a new task: update
+`README.md` first (or in the same change), then `shippingVat`/`priceOrder` and their tests.
